If the development server is not running, mikasa will start `scripts/serve-dev.js` in the background and open the browser to `http://localhost:8765`. It checks whether the port is already in use before starting a new instance, using `out/html/.dev-server.pid` to track the process ID. For simplicity, mikasa can also directly launch the server and open the browser without checking for existing processes.

The `serve-dev.js` script runs on `127.0.0.1:8765`, but on Windows with WSL, `localhost` may not resolve correctly unless configured properly in the system settings.

User on WSL with Windows Firefox may face issues accessing `localhost:8765` directly, as it might not reach the WSL server. Instead, they should use `http://$(hostname -I | awk '{print $1}'):8765` or ensure `localhost` is properly forwarded through WSL2 mirrored networking. On newer Windows 11 systems, `localhost:8765` often works automatically, but this isn't guaranteed across all setups.

For local development, using `file://` with cache-busting query strings is the safest approach, avoiding networking issues. Assets like `index.html` and `game.js` should include version stamps (e.g., `index.html?v=stamp` and `game.js?v=stamp`) when served via `file://`.

The `inject-dev-cache-meta.js` script will be expanded to handle cache busting comprehensively, including updating `mikasa-wsl.bashrc` and fixing an early-return bug that previously skipped cache busting on re-runs.

